---
/* The five words this family of archives grades a claim with, and the one
   construct that refuses to grade it at all. Identical in all seven archives,
   so that a level means the same thing wherever a reader meets it.

   documented  a record was read, and it says this
   probable    the evidence points one way and nothing points the other
   inferred    reasoned from what is known, not witnessed in a record
   family      told, remembered, or written on a family chart — not sourced
   disputed    two sources disagree and neither has been ruled out

   Older labels used by individual archives are folded in rather than broken.

   AND «family» IS NO LONGER WHERE UNKNOWN VALUES GO. It was, twice over: `no`
   and `open` were listed as aliases of it, and the fallback resolved anything
   unrecognised to it as well. So an archive passing `conn: "no"` — its own field
   for «is this person related at all», answered NO — got a badge reading
   «Family», in a column headed «Link to this family», against six people that
   archive explicitly states are not relatives. Piero della Francesca among them,
   two lines under a dek saying that a shared patronymic is not a shared ancestor
   and that saying otherwise is how family myths start.

   Nothing failed, which is why it stood. This component grades how well a claim
   is EVIDENCED, on five levels. `conn` asks whether a link exists at all, and
   «no» is an answer to that question rather than a weak grade on this one. A
   default that always returns a valid-looking answer cannot report that it was
   never given a valid input — and of the five levels available to fall back to,
   this one fell to the most credulous, in an estate whose whole purpose is not
   asserting kinship it cannot show.

   Unrecognised values now render «Ungraded» and say so. Not a build failure:
   several archives compute the level in an expression, so a throw here would
   turn a visible wrong word into seven broken builds, and the point is that the
   reader can see it. The Defranceski session found this and fixed their own
   misuse in 639d2a6 without touching the kit; this is the kit half. */
const { level, children } = Astro.props;

const ALIAS = {
  documented: "documented", doc: "documented", yes: "documented", proven: "documented",
  probable: "probable", likely: "probable",
  inferred: "inferred", inf: "inferred", unproven: "inferred", untested: "inferred",
  family: "family", lore: "family", tradition: "family", asserted: "family",
  disputed: "disputed", contested: "disputed", conflict: "disputed",
};
const LABEL = {
  documented: "Documented", probable: "Probable", inferred: "Inferred",
  family: "Family", disputed: "Disputed",
  ungraded: "Ungraded — this is not one of the five levels",
};
const key = ALIAS[String(level ?? "").toLowerCase()] ?? "ungraded";
---
<span class={`ev ev-${key}`} title={LABEL[key]}>{children ?? (key === "ungraded" ? "Ungraded" : LABEL[key])}</span>
